  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nRest of the Week: Warm and Dry\n\nA developing Omega Block across the Central CONUS will result \nin dry conditions for the remainder of the week. Surface high \npressure anchors over the Great Lakes in response to this ridge,\nresulting in drier continental air emanating eastward from this\ncell. As a result, dewpoints through the end of the week look \nto be quite pleasant in the 40s and 50s. Deeper mixing owing to \nthis drier airmass should result in highs reaching into the 80s \nfor most locales through the weekend. Did nudge highs down \nslightly for tomorrow below the deterministic NBM given the \ncooling 900-700-mb layer (maybe too much influence from the BC \nmodels?), but otherwise minimal changes were made to the \nforecast.\n\nEarly Next Week: Low Rain Chances, Still Warm\n\nA negatively-tilted shortwave trough impedes along the western \nflank of the Omega Block for Sunday with the ridge gradually \ndevolving through the first half of the week and transitioning \nto more southwesterly flow for the Upper Midwest. Several \nperturbations working through this flow could bring showers and \nstorms for the first half of the week, but the overall pattern \ndoesn't become more favorable for widespread showers until the \nlatter part of next week. Little change in the lower \ntropospheric airmass ensures that highs will reside in the 80s \nand lows in the 50s through the forecast period.",
